      "s6-c1-common",
      "Common Stock",
      "A stock is a small piece of a company. When people say \"stock,\" they usually mean common stock. Common stock is equity. Equity means ownership. If you own common stock, you own part of the company that issued it.\n\nWhy buy common stock? Most people buy it so their money can grow. Growth in the value of an investment is called capital appreciation. Stock owners also have limited liability. This means they are not personally responsible for the company's debts. The most they can lose is what they paid for their stock.\n\nThere is a trade-off. In general, the greater the chance of a big return, the greater the risk. Common stock is the most junior security. Junior means last in line. If the company goes bankrupt, taxes, debts, salaries, and other bills get paid first. Common shareholders split whatever is left, if anything.\n\nCommon stock is also usually the most volatile security. Volatile means the price can jump up and down a lot, even from one day to the next. Over time, common stock has given the greatest returns. The key words are \"over time.\" Common stock is a long-term investment. It is not a good fit for someone who needs the cash in the short or medium term.\n\nSome common stocks also pay dividends. A dividend is a share of the company's profits paid to owners. The board of directors decides whether to pay a dividend and how much. Shareholders vote for the board, but they do not vote on the amount or timing of dividends. Not every stock pays dividends. Those that do usually pay them every quarter (four times a year).",
